 Good. The deploy command asks the platform for what it needs:

```go
type Platform interface {
    // ... existing methods

    // Deploy lifecycle
    DeployCredentials(ctx context.Context) (DeployCredentials, error)
    StagingRegistry() string
    CanonicalRegistry() string
}

type DeployCredentials struct {
    MorselToken string
    RegistryAuth string
}
```

In `GCPPlatform`: `DeployCredentials()` exchanges the GitHub OIDC token for a Morsel token and returns GCP registry auth via WIF. `StagingRegistry()` returns the GCP Artifact Registry staging repo URL.

In `LocalPlatform`: `DeployCredentials()` reads the stored local profile token. `StagingRegistry()` returns the local in-cluster registry URL. No WIF, no OIDC exchange.

The deploy command itself has no platform-specific code:

```go
func Deploy(ctx context.Context, platform Platform) error {
    apps := discoverApps(".morsel/")
    creds, _ := platform.DeployCredentials(ctx)

    sync(creds.MorselToken, appNames(apps))

    parallel(apps, func(app App) {
        image := build(app)
        push(platform.StagingRegistry(), creds.RegistryAuth, image)
        deploy(creds.MorselToken, app, image)
    })
}
```

Same code path for local and CI. Platform handles all the credential and registry differences.

The environment variables the workflow sets are also platform-agnostic from the CLI's perspective — the CLI asks the platform how to interpret them. `GCPPlatform` reads `MORSEL_WIF_PROVIDER` and `MORSEL_SERVICE_ACCOUNT`; a future `AWSPlatform` would read different variables. The workflow template stays the same regardless of platform since the CLI mediates everything.

Shall I write up the full workflow template and update the spec with the extended `Platform` interface, then we can move to the admin UI design?

